WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.520
The Justice League is vaporized in a&nbsp;
single blast and Mr. Fantastic ends up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:11.220
a pile of blue spaghetti. It's been a rough&nbsp;
year for superheroes. Here's who's dead.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.800
Like his sons Thor and Loki, Odin appears&nbsp;
in Marvel comics and movies, DC comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:22.720
various works of fantasy, and, of course, Norse&nbsp;
mythology. MCU viewers associate the All-Father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.560
with Anthony Hopkins' portrayal in the first three&nbsp;
"Thor" movies. As is the case with Asgard itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:34.800
Marvel's Odin goes through cycles of destruction&nbsp;
and rebirth. He dematerializes into particles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.760
of bright light at the beginning of "Thor:&nbsp;
Ragnarok," and similarly wills himself out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:46.160
of existence at the conclusion of 2022's "Thor"&nbsp;
#22, by writer Donny Cates and artist Nic Klein.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.640
"I'm on a different path now.&nbsp;
This you must face alone."

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.400
In this penultimate installment of Cates' "God&nbsp;
of Hammers" arc, Odin reveals that his continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:02.480
existence is preventing Thor, the current king&nbsp;
of Asgard, from wielding the full cosmic might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:08.000
usually at the disposal of his royal station.&nbsp;
Recognizing that Thor needs an extra boost to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.680
defeat the renegade God of Hammers, a blend&nbsp;
of Mjolnir's energy and the nefarious Mangog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.340
Odin releases the last of his power to&nbsp;
his son, erasing himself in the process.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:26.400
In 2016's "Suicide Squad," only two members&nbsp;
of Task Force X bite the big one. In contrast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:33.200
2021's "The Suicide Squad" includes a wholesale&nbsp;
massacre of DC C-list baddies. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.720
this slaughter does not include Peacemaker,&nbsp;
allowing him to return for his HBO Max series.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:43.200
Perhaps in the interest of consistency&nbsp;
with the film, "Peacemaker" ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.600
Season 1 with fewer characters than it&nbsp;
starts. Most of these characters are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.640
ordinary humans or members of the hostile&nbsp;
alien race colloquially known as butterflies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.360
The butterfly Ik Nobe Llok, who inhabits&nbsp;
the body of ex-mercenary Clemson Murn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.880
is sort of both. And in many respects, his&nbsp;
ultimately self-sacrificing quest to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.920
his fellow butterflies from annihilating&nbsp;
the human race qualifies as super-heroic.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.840
After the butterflies take over the&nbsp;
police force of Evergreen, Washington,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.880
Murn throws himself under the metaphorical&nbsp;
bus to allow Harcourt and Adebayo to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.280
evade execution and carry on with&nbsp;
their mission. In a tragic twist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:27.280
the butterfly Eek Stack Ik Ik — sometimes referred&nbsp;
to as "Goff" and inhabiting the body of Detective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:32.508
Sophie Song — happens to be the individual&nbsp;
who repeatedly pulls the trigger on Murn.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.000
We haven't seen a ton of super-pets&nbsp;
since 1985's "Crisis on Infinite Earths"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.040
reset the DC universe's timeline and removed them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:46.160
from officially-recognized existence. So&nbsp;
it's nice to see Krypto the Superdog and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.520
Comet the Super-Horse make something of a&nbsp;
comeback in 2021's "Supergirl: Woman of Tomorrow."

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:57.120
The eight-issue epic sends Kara Zor-El on a&nbsp;
galaxy-spanning journey. She's determined to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.720
track down Krem, an interstellar pirate who&nbsp;
needlessly slew her friend's father, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:07.120
poisoned Krypto while making his escape.&nbsp;
By the time the final issue arrives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:12.080
Supergirl has successfully detained Krem.&nbsp;
However, Krem manages to send for a cavalry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.560
of space pirates to help reverse his&nbsp;
fortune. After a significant struggle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.360
Supergirl manages to dispatch these baddies,&nbsp;
but Comet loses his life in the process.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.760
It's a little ambiguous whether "Supergirl:&nbsp;
Woman of Tomorrow" is a canonical tale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.680
so this Comet's precise nature&nbsp;
is difficult to pin down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.240
But Supergirl does mention that she knew&nbsp;
he was a man cursed to live as a horse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:41.360
a fact he didn't love talking about. This death&nbsp;
might not stick, but it's still enormously moving.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:47.600
Netflix threw its hat into the superhero satire&nbsp;
ring with 2022's "The Guardians of Justice."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:53.040
Reminiscent of "The Boys," "Invincible," and&nbsp;
HBO's "Watchmen," "The Guardians of Justice"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:59.120
doesn't treat the cape-and-cowl set as spotless&nbsp;
heroes. Marvelous Man, this universe's Superman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:04.880
equivalent, seemingly dies live on television,&nbsp;
but some of his colleagues suspect foul play.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:09.840
"I'm tired. So I found my escape."

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.423
"No. Don't do this, Cal!"

00:04:11.423 --> 00:04:13.120
"I found it."

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.040
Much like Rorschach's search for the&nbsp;
Comedian's killer kicks off "Watchmen,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.640
"The Guardians of Justice" joins Knight Hawk as&nbsp;
he explores the mystery of Marvelous Man's death.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.520
But who is Marvelous Man? He arrived on Earth&nbsp;
after his home planet was destroyed by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:33.120
Brainiac-like villain. Like any wannabe Superman&nbsp;
worth his salt, Marvelous Man can fly, has heat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:38.800
vision, boasts seemingly impossible strength, and&nbsp;
is virtually invulnerable to all potential harm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:44.480
except for this world's Kryptonite equivalent.&nbsp;
The copyright-safe version of a Kryptonite bullet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:50.240
that kills him is ostensibly stolen from a&nbsp;
stand-in for Lex Luthor. Sound bizarre? It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:55.840
is. But that's "The Guardians of Justice" in&nbsp;
a copyright-safe, kryptonite-laced nutshell.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.800
In 2022's "The Human Target," private&nbsp;
investigator Christopher Chance, aka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.360
the Human Target, ingests a slow-acting&nbsp;
poison intended for Lex Luthor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:09.680
He has 12 days to figure out who's to&nbsp;
blame for his rapidly approaching death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.080
and the hardboiled gumshoe suspects the&nbsp;
involvement of at least one member of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:19.440
Justice League International. The ensuing tale&nbsp;
blends elements of noir detective traditions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.960
the irreverent side of DC publishing that&nbsp;
emerged in the late 1980s and early 1990s.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:29.680
Whether or not Tom King and Greg Smallwood's&nbsp;
miniseries takes place in the mainstream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.800
DC timeline is unclear. Therefore,&nbsp;
Guy Gardner may appear in another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.840
more canonical story before the end of the&nbsp;
year, or even reemerge somehow in "The Human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.400
Target." Nevertheless, the fact remains&nbsp;
that the Green Lantern Corps' biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.600
jerk is killed in a fight with Ice, his ex,&nbsp;
and Chris Chance in "The Human Target" #6.

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:56.400
Originally one of Christopher's suspects, subzero&nbsp;
superheroine Ice eventually becomes his romantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.840
interest, much to Guy's jealous fury. The&nbsp;
third-most famous Green Lantern attacks the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:07.520
new couple after a night of passion, only to find&nbsp;
himself frozen solid and shattered to pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:12.320
in that order. Let that be a lesson&nbsp;
to awful exes everywhere, nothing good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:18.000
ever comes from stalking and harassment,&nbsp;
even if you have a power ring from space.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:22.560
Plenty of folks who'd like to get into&nbsp;
superhero comics find themselves stymied by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:29.280
DC and Marvel's convoluted continuity, jarring&nbsp;
reboots, and reliance upon obscure characters with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.920
omnipotent powers appearing from out of&nbsp;
nowhere. They've got a new hurdle to clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:39.920
courtesy of "Justice League #75" by Joshua&nbsp;
Williamson and Rafa Sandoval. In this issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:45.760
a previously benevolent dimensional traveler&nbsp;
named Pariah effortlessly dematerializes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:51.680
sizeable fraction of DC's best-known,&nbsp;
usually-unstoppable champions of virtue.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:56.960
Specifically, Pariah kills the Justice League.&nbsp;
Superman, Batman, Wonder Woman, Green Lantern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:02.960
Zatanna, Aquaman, Hawkgirl, and the Martian&nbsp;
Manhunter all evaporate into nothing. He also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:08.000
bumps off multidimensional Justice Leaguers&nbsp;
including President Superman of Earth-23,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:13.600
Doctor Multiverse of Earth-8, Captain&nbsp;
Carrot of Earth-23, and a handful of others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:17.680
Promotional materials tell us this&nbsp;
ghastly, traumatizing massacre of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:23.360
globally beloved characters leads into&nbsp;
DC's summer of 2022 event, "Dark Crisis."

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.800
All of this is plenty shocking by itself, but&nbsp;
here's the kicker, current "Action Comics"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.840
scribe Phillip Kennedy Johnson has already&nbsp;
made it known, via an interview with CBR,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:39.280
that Superman's death will in no way interrupt&nbsp;
Kal-El's noble struggle against intergalactic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:44.480
tyranny and oppression in his concurrent&nbsp;
"Warworld Saga." Likewise, ceasing to exist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:49.360
doesn't seem to be stopping Batman from hunting&nbsp;
Ra's al Ghul's assassin in the "Shadow War"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:55.360
multi-series crossover. DC's most prominent heroes&nbsp;
have died in "Justice League." Somehow, they all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:01.172
continue to prosper in their solo adventures.&nbsp;
What gives? We suppose we'll have to keep reading.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:07.040
Fans of the Fantastic Four have waited years for&nbsp;
Marvel's First Family to take their rightful place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:11.760
at the center of the MCU, and they probably&nbsp;
won't have to stay patient for much longer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:15.760
Even though it's currently in need of a&nbsp;
new director, "Marvel's Fantastic Four"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:20.880
should come to fruition within the not-so-distant&nbsp;
future. Hopefully the version of Reed Richards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:24.960
from the mainstream MCU timeline fares a&nbsp;
little better than the Reed who leads the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:30.560
Illuminati from Earth-838 because that&nbsp;
guy dies a swift and humiliating death.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:34.800
In "Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of&nbsp;
Madness," the Scarlet Witch chases Steven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:40.880
Strange and America Chavez to Earth-838, hoping to&nbsp;
use America's dimension-hopping powers to reunite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:45.920
with her sons. Earth-838's Illuminati,&nbsp;
a secretive group of elite superheroes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:51.840
do not initially take the threat of the Scarlet&nbsp;
Witch seriously. If they can beat Thanos, how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:57.840
of a problem could Wanda Maximoff possibly be,&nbsp;
right? As it happens, the Scarlet Witch controls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:04.536
reality itself, so she turns Reed into string&nbsp;
cheese and then makes his brains explode.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:10.800
The Inhumans TV show is the biggest abject&nbsp;
failure in MCU history, and there isn't a close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:15.600
second. The X-Men's movie rights returning&nbsp;
to Marvel pretty much destroys any remaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:20.080
chance of the Inhumans occupying the MCU's&nbsp;
lane for genetically gifted outcasts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:25.760
And Black Bolt probably died the most&nbsp;
gruesome and unsettling demise of any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:30.080
Illuminati member in "Multiverse of&nbsp;
Madness," which is saying something.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:36.240
After Reed Richards of Earth-838 informs Wanda&nbsp;
that Black Bolt can annihilate her with a whisper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:38.720
Wanda simply smirks and uses her magic.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:39.860
"What mouth?"

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:45.920
The king of the Inhumans then notices&nbsp;
that his mouth has vanished. He panics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:52.160
and the resulting yelp causes his skull to pop&nbsp;
like a blister. We don't think the mainstream MCU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:58.560
timeline's Black Bolt, also played by Anson Mount,&nbsp;
would ever be dumb enough to use his unfathomably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:04.080
destructive vocal cords while his mouth was&nbsp;
sewn shut. He might be a commercial failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:10.236
but the Black Bolt of the ABC television network&nbsp;
is probably way harder to kill than this guy.

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:14.400
Since arriving on the scene in 2011's&nbsp;
"Captain America: The First Avenger,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:18.880
Peggy Carter has journeyed from movies to&nbsp;
network television to streaming animation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:23.680
While anyone who watched ABC's "Agent Carter"&nbsp;
can attest to the combat prowess of the version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:29.040
of Peggy who resides in the regular MCU timeline,&nbsp;
the Peggy variant from "What if... Captain Carter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:33.680
Were the First Avenger?" would probably be&nbsp;
more useful in a fight against Dr. Doom.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:38.880
The Disney+ series "What If...?" tells us the&nbsp;
tale of Captain Carter, a time-displaced World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:43.840
War II-era hero who's injected with the super&nbsp;
soldier-serum when the original test subject,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:48.080
Steve Rogers, is made temporarily&nbsp;
unavailable. However, we can safely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:53.680
presume the events of "What If...?" are not&nbsp;
directly connected to conditions on Earth-838.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:58.000
Captain Carter is slated for significant&nbsp;
amounts of screen time in "What If...?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:04.560
Season 2, and the Earth-838 variant of Peggy is&nbsp;
all out of time, screen-related or otherwise.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:09.440
Relative to the other Illuminati members, Captain&nbsp;
Carter puts up a decent fight against the Scarlet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:14.800
Witch. She even manages to land a few strikes&nbsp;
before Wanda takes telepathic control of Captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:20.888
Carter's signature shield and uses it to separate&nbsp;
Peggy's lower half from the rest of her torso.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:26.560
In the primary MCU timeline, Maria Rambeau&nbsp;
is Carol Danvers' friend, and the mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:31.920
of Monica Rambeau. She sadly passes away during&nbsp;
the years between "Infinity War" and "Endgame,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:37.680
but not before making a big impact in the world&nbsp;
of the MCU by founding S.W.O.R.D. In Earth-838,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:42.880
Maria became Captain Marvel instead of Carol&nbsp;
Danvers, and she went on to join Reed Richards'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:48.160
Illuminati. Sharing the attitude of the rest of&nbsp;
her pompous secret society, Maria tells Strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:53.440
they "can handle your little witch" if Wanda&nbsp;
Maximoff shows up looking for America Chavez.

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:57.360
As it turns out, Earth-838's&nbsp;
Captain Marvel can't handle the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:02.000
"little witch," who squashes Captain&nbsp;
Marvel by dropping a statue on her.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:08.160
Somewhat curiously, Earth-838 Maria wears a&nbsp;
teal-colored costume similar to the uniform Carol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:14.720
Danvers fights in during her time as the amnesiac&nbsp;
Kree warrior Vers in 2019's "Captain Marvel."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:18.960
In theory, this could indicate that&nbsp;
some sort of diplomatic relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:21.920
exists between the Kree and&nbsp;
the governments of Earth-838,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:26.004
but who knows if we'll ever hear&nbsp;
more about this timeline's history?

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:30.960
Patrick Stewart played Professor Charles&nbsp;
Xavier for the first time back in the year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:37.680
2000's "X-Men" and once said he'd step away from&nbsp;
the role after the high note of 2017's "Logan."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:41.760
Apparently, Kevin Feige used mind control&nbsp;
abilities of his own to convince Stewart to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:45.760
play Xavier for the eighth time in&nbsp;
"Multiverse of Madness." Although,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:51.120
in fairness, since the Earth-838 variant of&nbsp;
the benevolent mutant telepath technically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:56.480
isn't the same person we see leading the X-Men&nbsp;
in any 20th Century Studios "X-Men" movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:00.480
Stewart could somewhat credibly argue&nbsp;
that he never went back on his word.

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:05.760
Like the rest of the Illuminati, Earth-838&nbsp;
Professor X finds himself utterly overwhelmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:10.000
by the Scarlet Witch, who creeps up&nbsp;
behind him while he's inside Wanda's mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:15.920
and snaps his neck like a celery stick. We&nbsp;
wish we could've seen more of Professor X&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:20.800
in the "Multiverse of Madness," especially&nbsp;
after Stewart's authoritative declaration of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:25.376
"we should tell him the truth" was&nbsp;
so heavily featured in trailers.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:31.600
Unpredictable explosions of grotesque violence&nbsp;
are a huge part of the appeal of Amazon Prime's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:37.040
"The Boys." Based on the remains that Homelander&nbsp;
shows to Starlight in "Glorious Five Year Plan,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:41.120
it's hard to tell exactly what the&nbsp;
leader of the Seven did to Supersonic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:47.440
It looks like Homelander punched his face into his&nbsp;
skull and cut his leg off with his heat vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:53.120
but we can only speculate on the precise&nbsp;
nature of Supersonic's multiple fatal injuries.

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:57.920
Years before winning the American Hero reality&nbsp;
show competition and joining the Seven,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:02.000
Supersonic had been Starlight's first&nbsp;
boyfriend, and nothing we see on "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:06.720
Boys" indicates that he was anything&nbsp;
short of a perfect gentleman. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:11.920
his friendship with Starlight and his principled&nbsp;
inclination towards doing the right thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:15.840
is exactly what ultimately lands&nbsp;
him in Homelander's crosshairs.

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:18.388
"Stop it. You know how that ends."

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:24.000
Little is known of the winged supe known as&nbsp;
Swatto, other than he was a member of Payback and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:29.920
a close associate of Solider Boy during the&nbsp;
1980s. While his demise was needless and tragic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:32.640
it provides a teachable moment for the rest of us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:37.360
It's important to remember certain abilities are&nbsp;
less useful than others in specific situations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:43.280
In this instance, while the power of hovering&nbsp;
flight is handy for rescuing cats from trees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:48.080
it’s utterly worthless against&nbsp;
enemies wielding anti-air artillery.

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:53.200
We know Payback's 1984 mission in Nicaragua is&nbsp;
primarily notable for the disappearance of Soldier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:59.280
Boy and subsequent government cover-up. However,&nbsp;
the fiasco also marks the final moments of Swatto.

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:05.760
The Green Lantern of Sector 2813 made his first&nbsp;
comic book appearance in the early 1960s and has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:11.040
remained a consistent supporting cast member&nbsp;
in comics, animated series, and video games.

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:16.320
Tomar-Re is fairly significant relative to&nbsp;
the hundreds of other DC characters who've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:20.720
been created over the years. And talking&nbsp;
about him as if he might as well be a red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:25.600
shirt from "Star Trek: The Original Series" is&nbsp;
a little preposterous. Nevertheless, it happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:31.200
to be the case that the "Young Justice" version of&nbsp;
Tomar-Re is killed battling Lor-Zod on New Genesis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:36.080
in the Season 4 episode, "Odyssey of Death!"&nbsp;
While he's introduced a few episodes earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:39.920
he doesn't get a ton of time in the&nbsp;
spotlight before the curtains fall.

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:46.000
That said, he makes the most of the few moments&nbsp;
he does have onscreen. Tomar-Re explains that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:50.640
his failure to prevent the destruction&nbsp;
of Krypton has haunted him for decades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:56.560
See, he knew and admired Superman's biological&nbsp;
father, Jor-El. While Tomar-Re himself doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:02.240
survive Lor-Zod's assault, he does manage to&nbsp;
save New Genesis from sharing Krypton's fate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:06.000
wraps up his tenure in the universe&nbsp;
with a little bit of redemption.

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:09.440
One of the ex-Payback members&nbsp;
who betrays Soldier Boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:14.560
Crimson Countess might be one of the most bearable&nbsp;
affiliates of the original Vought super-team.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:19.840
Naturally, there's some degree of narcissism&nbsp;
and a savior complex motivating her advocacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:24.640
for endangered chimpanzees, but at least&nbsp;
she channels her personality shortcomings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:29.920
in a positive direction. Though the fact that&nbsp;
these days she calls a small trailer her home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:33.760
perhaps her one true weakness&nbsp;
is sound financial investing.

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:38.400
Speaking of her trailer, that's where&nbsp;
she's blasted off the face of existence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:42.720
by an exploding Soldier Boy, a fate&nbsp;
shared by numerous characters in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:46.640
third season of Amazon's pitch-black&nbsp;
superhero satire. Maybe it's a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:51.120
cold to turn your boyfriend over to hostile&nbsp;
military scientists, but at the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:54.968
wasn't helping put Soldier Boy out&nbsp;
of commission the right thing to do?

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:58.240
We don't spend a ton of time with&nbsp;
Blue Hawk on "The Boys." He's only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:02.160
in a few episodes and mostly exists to&nbsp;
serve as a ruthless commentary on how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:06.320
American police have handled the deaths&nbsp;
of George Floyd and numerous others.

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:12.480
"I go where the crime is, and the crime&nbsp;
just happens to be in Black neighborhoods."

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:19.200
Blue Hawk swears he's not racist, but he also&nbsp;
insists that hip hop doesn't count as real music.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:23.920
He went all the way down the birtherism&nbsp;
rabbit hole during the Obama administration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:29.920
and frequently bemoans the perceived unfairness&nbsp;
of his inability to say racial slurs in public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:35.280
without social consequences. A-Train has&nbsp;
made some unfortunate choices over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:39.600
but breaking the sound barrier while&nbsp;
dragging Blue Hawk across the pavement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:42.812
probably isn't on his list of regrets.

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:46.960
The TNT Twins were former members of&nbsp;
Payback who, when they held hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:52.560
could shoot lightning-like bursts at baddies, but&nbsp;
that's definitely not what we remember them for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:57.840
More memorably, the TNT Twins are the&nbsp;
hosts of Herogasm in "The Boys" Season 3.

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:00.480
As we see from a flashback to the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:05.040
they were essentially useless in serious&nbsp;
combat situations. Back in their heyday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:09.760
the TNT Twins shot a little laser that made&nbsp;
a big noise and could knock people over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.760 --> 00:18:15.520
meaning virtually any ranged weapon would've been&nbsp;
more effective than their so-called superpowers.

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:19.840
As a result, they functioned a little more&nbsp;
like television personalities than what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:24.720
typically think of as superheroes. By the time&nbsp;
Soldier Boy catches up to them at Herogasm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:29.680
their powers no longer work at all due to&nbsp;
years of neglect. By the end of their lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:34.720
Tommy and Tessa are more like aging circus&nbsp;
performers than one-time crime fighters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:37.680
So even if you don't approve&nbsp;
of their lifestyle choices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:43.120
one has to wonder if they really deserve to&nbsp;
be victims of Soldier Boy's homicidal rampage.

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:47.600
In the latest "Thor" movie, Jane Foster&nbsp;
is dying of cancer while she devotes her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:52.480
dwindling existence to defending innocent&nbsp;
people from all manner of cosmic threats.

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:55.520
"I just want to say that was very very&nbsp;
impressive what you did back there."

00:18:55.520 --> 00:19:01.520
However, "Thor: Love and Thunder" is only partly&nbsp;
Jane's movie, for pretty understandable reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:06.400
Chris Hemsworth is still very much the star&nbsp;
of the show. In the continuity of the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:10.240
Jane spent several years away from the&nbsp;
spotlight, but the latest film in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:15.200
franchise sees her triumphant return, this&nbsp;
time as more than a basic love interest.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:20.960
Sometime after her experiences in 2011's "Thor"&nbsp;
and 2013's "Thor: The Dark World," Jane Foster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:26.800
breaks up with intergalactically acknowledged hunk&nbsp;
Thor. Following a diagnosis of late-stage cancer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:32.960
Jane finds herself drawn to the Asgardian hammer&nbsp;
Mjolnir, which declares her worthy and transforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:38.560
her into the Mighty Thor. Tragically, the magic&nbsp;
hammer is unable to cure her illness. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:43.360
using the weapon is actually hurting her. But she&nbsp;
chooses to pick up Mjolnir and save her Asgardian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.360 --> 00:19:49.040
boyfriend right as he's about to meet his fate.&nbsp;
As a result of her selfless decision, she dies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:54.988
shortly after the final confrontation with Gorr&nbsp;
the God Butcher, and is welcomed to Valhalla.

00:19:55.280 --> 00:20:00.904
While we lost numerous heroes, a few&nbsp;
villains also didn't survive 2022.

00:20:01.040 --> 00:20:06.080
With so many supervillains hatching&nbsp;
so many devious plots at all times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:10.640
you'd think there'd be no original&nbsp;
scheme for world domination left untried.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:15.920
Orm the Ocean Master from "Young Justice"&nbsp;
is basically Loki, with far less charisma,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:20.480
but let's give him credit, he attempts what&nbsp;
truly seems to be an original evil plan.

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:25.600
Correctly guessing he could charm the people&nbsp;
of Atlantis by embodying an imagined glorious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:30.400
past, Ocean Master transplants his&nbsp;
consciousness into a clone of Arion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:35.920
the original leader of the undersea kingdom.&nbsp;
Ocean Master wins major battles and makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:40.320
plenty of inspiring speeches. To cover&nbsp;
his bases, he also clones himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:46.560
and loads that clone up with memories, so only&nbsp;
an expert mind reader could sniff out his ruse.

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:49.920
"This is your chance to redeem&nbsp;
yourself before the light."

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:52.720
"And achieve everything I have ever dreamt of."

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:59.040
As it happens, the "Young Justice" crew includes&nbsp;
prominent telepath M'gann M'orzz , who easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:04.320
figures out what's up. But by the time M'gann&nbsp;
warns the Atlanteans of Ocean Master's presence in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:10.080
Arion's body at the end of Season 4's "Leviathan&nbsp;
Wakes," it's too late. "Arion" is declared the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:15.280
new king of Atlantis. Luckily, thanks to an&nbsp;
ancient prophecy involving the Lords of Order and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.280 --> 00:21:21.632
Vandal Savage, "Arion" spectacularly explodes&nbsp;
the instant he puts the crown on his head.

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:26.560
Longtime arch-nemesis to Bruce Wayne&nbsp;
and scary grandpa to Damian Wayne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:29.760
Ra's al Ghul is assassinated at the onset of DC's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:34.160
"Shadow War" event, causing plenty&nbsp;
of readers to roll their eyes.

00:21:34.160 --> 00:21:39.680
Not only does Ra's, the centuries-old one-time&nbsp;
leader of the League of Assassins, benefit from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.680 --> 00:21:44.560
the same squishy relationship with mortality&nbsp;
that keeps the rest of DC and Marvel's denizens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:49.360
uniquely prone to resurrections, immortality&nbsp;
is kind of his whole deal. In the past, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:55.120
injuries or illness had Ra's skirting the edges of&nbsp;
the great beyond, he'd retreat to the Lazarus Pit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:59.840
a literal fountain of youth, and regenerate&nbsp;
his body into that of a younger, healthier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:05.600
megalomaniacal eco-terrorist. Basically, Ra's al&nbsp;
Ghul dying and not coming back is kind of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:12.000
if the Joker stopped doing murders and laughing&nbsp;
at ironically morbid circumstances. It just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:17.360
feels wrong even if it's clearly a positive&nbsp;
development for the citizens of Gotham City.

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:23.760
But implausible as it seems, after "Shadow War," a&nbsp;
multi-title crossover penned by Joshua Williamson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:27.680
Ra's al Ghul remains as apparently&nbsp;
deceased as he does at the beginning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:31.440
Damian is still pretty messed up&nbsp;
about the death of Alfred Pennyworth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:36.820
and now he's lost his literal grandfather&nbsp;
shortly after his surrogate grandfather.

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:42.160
When the mercenary forces of Talia al Ghul&nbsp;
and Deathstroke throw down in "Shadow War,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:47.120
a lot of them die, including Respawn. The&nbsp;
promising young killing machine debuts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.120 --> 00:22:51.120
in the League of Lazarus deathmatch&nbsp;
tournament seen in the pages of 2021's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:56.480
"Robin." He initially appears to have lifted&nbsp;
his costume style and gimmick from Deathstroke.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:23:00.880
But Respawn is much more than a&nbsp;
Slade Wilson fanboy. Ra's al Ghul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.880 --> 00:23:03.760
being an utterly insane scientific genius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:09.680
mixed genetic material from his daughter Talia&nbsp;
and Deathstroke to create the mercenary. Damian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.680 --> 00:23:15.120
Wayne is initially psyched to find out he has a&nbsp;
half-brother, but his excitement is short-lived.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.120 --> 00:23:19.440
Shortly after the two meet, Respawn hurls&nbsp;
himself in front of a hail of bullets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:25.992
and various other projectile weapons intended for&nbsp;
Slade, and as a result, he is ripped to shreds.

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:31.520
Over the years, "Young Justice" has done&nbsp;
a spectacular job establishing its unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.520 --> 00:23:38.240
vision of the Teen Titans, but its takes on the&nbsp;
DC Universe's big bad guys tend to feel a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:43.440
traditional. The Light is a pretty faithful&nbsp;
version of the Legion of Doom. Villains linked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:50.160
with Markovia and Apokolips routinely appear in&nbsp;
other DC animated projects, and the General Zod of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:55.760
"Young Justice: Phantoms," to his credit, is not a&nbsp;
major departure from Terence Stamp's Zod of yore.

00:23:56.800 --> 00:24:00.960
“Come to me, son of Jor-El. Kneel before Zod!"

00:24:01.520 --> 00:24:06.880
But that brings us to Lor-Zod, elder&nbsp;
Zod's savvy and cold-blooded son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:12.320
a profoundly dangerous foil for Superboy&nbsp;
and the Legion of Super-Heroes. Lor-Zod&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:17.840
feels like a deranged sociopath who is&nbsp;
specific to "Young Justice." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:24.480
Season 4 ends with Lor-Zod teleporting into a&nbsp;
bomb blast on Mars that vaporizes him immediately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:29.120
but that doesn't mean we'll never see him&nbsp;
again. He is a time traveler, after all.

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:31.520
Even if Lor-Zod is permanently toast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.520 --> 00:24:36.240
the final moments of "Phantoms" lead us&nbsp;
to believe a hypothetical next season will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:40.826
definitely not share the uninteresting&nbsp;
villain problems of previous seasons.

